{# The site navigation, in ONE place.

   Before the 7-page split this list was hardcoded three times — the header, the
   footer, and the static 404 — as in-page anchors (`#about`, `#menu`, …). Three
   copies of six links is three chances to forget one, and the static site had
   already drifted. It is now generated from Grav's own page tree, so adding a
   page in the admin adds it to both navs and nothing has to be edited here.

   ORDER comes from the folder's numeric prefix (03.okrestnosti, 04.kuhnya, …),
   which is also the order the client sees in Pages. Renumber there, not here.

   WHAT APPEARS: `.visible` is Grav's own test — a folder with a numeric prefix
   is visible unless its front matter says otherwise. 01.home and 02.privacy
   both set `visible: false`, because the logo already goes home and the privacy
   link lives in the footer's bottom bar. A new page WILL appear here the moment
   the client creates it; that is the intended behaviour, not an oversight.

   LABEL is `p.menu`, which Grav falls back to `p.title` for. Every page sets a
   short one-word `menu:` so seven of them fit on one row.

   `p.active` is Grav's — the highlight is rendered server-side. The scrollspy
   that used to do this in JS was deleted with the anchors: it matched
   `href === '#' + id`, so with real URLs no link could ever match, and because
   it ran `toggle` on every intersection it would have actively stripped this
   class off again. #}
{% for p in pages.children.visible %}
  <a href="{{ p.url }}"{% if p.active %} class="is-active" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ p.menu }}</a>
{% endfor %}
